# Trak — MVP Requirements (first pilot)

Scope for the first real-child pilot, incorporating the 23 September decisions
and the 7–8 October 2026 events decision. This file defines the required
behavior; it does not certify that every requirement has shipped or passed.
Deployed evidence belongs in [PILOT-INDEX](docs/use-cases/PILOT-INDEX.md).
Assignments and acceptance evidence belong on the corresponding Linear issue.
Changes follow [the release gate](docs/release/merge-gate.md).

The April pilot wedge and REQ-001–005 are historical. Their replacement is the
J1–J8 contract below. Formal original scope acceptance is tracked in
[TRAK-21](https://linear.app/trak-football/issue/TRAK-21); the events addition is
[TRAK-25](https://linear.app/trak-football/issue/TRAK-25).
The independent scope review remains tracked in
[TRAK-39](https://linear.app/trak-football/issue/TRAK-39).

## Pilot boundary

One whole squad at one UAE academy, for eight weeks. The academy chooses the
squad. The agreed start date, cohort count and contacts must be recorded before
admission; a provisional configured date is not permission to start. Rehearse
with at least the required 25-player synthetic squad. Do not publish the
academy's identity or private meeting links in this repository.

The loop is academy admission → guardian consent → child activation → coach
schedules and runs a session → coach records attendance, assesses and writes a
message → player sees the message and bands; linked parents see bands and
history. Events are part of the first pilot, not a feature to add after launch.

## Required journeys

### J1 — Academy admission

The academy supplies child name, date of birth, age group, assigned coach and
every guardian email. A child's own email is optional. Trak loads the verified
roster through the concierge workflow; the academy console remains coming soon.

Only rostered children and academy-supplied guardians are admitted. The backend
refuses uninvited player and parent profiles. Children do not join by entering a
name or coach code, and coaches do not add children. A player cannot change
their own date of birth; missing date of birth counts as under 18.

The roster is the source of the child's name throughout setup and the app.
Trak corrects roster identity/contact errors through the audited operator
procedure, not by asking the child to overwrite them.

### J2 — Parent consent

Guardian invitations are sent when the child is admitted. They must arrive at
Gmail and Outlook in under 60 seconds, in the inbox rather than spam, and name
the child, academy and purpose with Trak branding and a safe-sender line. An
existing guardian's email for another child must name that child too.

The required consent covers coaching records, assessments, the published
message and viewing by the child and linked parents. Record the guardian's
name, relationship and notice version. Parents see bands and history, not the
coach's message. The screen must promise only choices the system enforces.

Each guardian has their own sign-in. One guardian can approve siblings without
a second account. Withdrawal is one tap from the signed-in parent profile and
blocks processing and family access, including sessions already open.

**Open implementation/notice discrepancy:** at the audited main `d0ed55f`, the
consent screen also records optional parent-visibility and recognition choices.
Family read access does not depend on the parent-visibility choice, and
recognition authoring is parked. This must be reconciled with the required
consent wording and counsel-approved notice; existing J2 rehearsal results do
not establish that those optional choices control access.

### J3 — Child activation and recovery

Consent is required before either activation path:

- With a child email, send a one-use invitation to the rostered address. First
  send, resend, expiry, wrong account and existing account must behave safely.
  Only the newest code works after a resend. Recovery uses that email.
- Without a child email, the approved guardian creates the child's username
  and password, then resets the password through their own account if needed.
  A guardian reset invalidates existing child sessions; an open child device
  must sign out within the accepted 30-second limit.

Invite, sign-in and reset emails display a code entered at `/auth/code`. No
button or fallback in those emails may consume the credential before the person
enters it. Signup confirmation is a separate `/auth/confirm` flow; it deliberately
signs out after confirmation and is not the pilot family's invitation path.
See TRAK-84, TRAK-103, TRAK-104, TRAK-107 and TRAK-117.

### J4 — Coach records a completed session

- Match: date, opponent, result, and each child's position, minutes, goals and
  assists, without invented defaults.
- Training: date, type, attendance and session notes.
- Every write checks active consent and the coach's academy assignment.
- Retries must not duplicate records or silently lose entered data.

The existing match/session paths remain in scope. J8's conversion of a past
event into a completed session must feed the same attendance and assessment
workflow rather than create an incompatible second record.

### J5 — Coach assessment and message

The squad marks each child ready to assess or waiting for a parent. A coach may
open the academy-supplied profile before consent, but cannot assess the player
until consent is active. Show the waiting reason plainly. This distinction was
confirmed on 8 October and replaces the older blanket restriction on opening
the profile. Assessment and development writes remain consent-gated. If consent is
withdrawn while a form is open, saving is refused and the entered values remain.

The assessment screen has six sliders, the resulting band, a message visible
only to the child, and an optional private note visible only to its coach. One
Save publishes the message; nothing reaches the player before saving. A partial
failure states what saved and what did not.

Assessments require a completed session attended by the player, with one
assessment per player per session. `/coach/assess` is the pilot assessment
screen; Quick Assess remains hidden. Parents never see the coach's message.
These decisions are recorded in TRAK-63, TRAK-64, TRAK-68 and TRAK-100.

### J6 — Player and parent see the records

Player home shows the latest band, six bars and coach message, marked new until
opened. Before the first
assessment it says the player is waiting for their coach. The player and linked
parent see coach-recorded match and training history. Parents see bands and
history for the selected child, never the coach's message or private notes.
Sibling switching must show only the selected child's data.

A failed load shows a clear error and retry control. It must not look like an
empty history. Shared-device, wrong-account and withdrawal cases remain part of
the deployed rehearsal.

### J7 — Measure actual use

An authorized operator must answer within one minute: assessments per coach
this week, and player and parent assessment opens this week. Count real UI
events for the configured pilot cohort; exclude synthetic accounts in pilot
reporting.

**Known measurement limit:** `feedback_opened` means player home displayed the
published message, not that the child deliberately opened or read it. Count
distinct player/assessment pairs per week. Parent `assessment_viewed` means
parent home displayed the latest bands and counts distinct parent/assessment
pairs. General app-opening retention is a separate metric. Do not describe
these as proof of reading. A better measure and numeric value targets remain
open. See TRAK-10 and TRAK-71.

### J8 — Events and family calendars

The coach schedules training, matches and other events with weekly repeat.
Players and parents see relevant events in the app and their phone calendar.
Changes and cancellations reach affected families. A past event becomes a
completed session with attendance. A per-academy off switch must require no
deployment. The latest 8 October TRAK-25 decision splits this into 18
implementation issues, TRAK-124–TRAK-141, all Todo when reviewed. The academy
console and AI schedule import remain out of scope.

The updated scope includes:

- Match kit as an event field. Lineups and yes/no/maybe matchday planning remain
  parked separately. For J8 attendance, everyone is expected unless a guardian
  reports “Can't make it”; the coach records actual absence after the event.
  The guardian can give an optional short reason and undo the response until
  the start. These responses are not a published family absence list.
- League fixtures imported from CSV or PDF. CSV is the first path; the PDF
  method remains open. AI extraction is outside the pilot (TRAK-129).
- A private calendar link per person, with replacement revoking the previous
  link, and one-tap subscription during signup or later from Settings. A feed
  fetch is not proof a phone has displayed its update (TRAK-132/133).
- Changes in the app and parent bell. Today/tomorrow changes use guardian
  email; player email remains open. Two-day reminders are grouped per guardian
  and day, with opt-out in Settings. Emails contain no unsubscribe link or
  one-click action that changes state when a scanner opens it (TRAK-134–136).
- A manual WhatsApp share action with event details and no child names or
  absence list. The coach chooses the destination and sends; automatic posting
  is out (TRAK-139).
- Separately revocable, named extra calendar links a guardian can give to a
  relative or driver, scoped to selected children's matches or all events.
  The recipient needs no Trak account. This planned sharing adds privacy and
  revocation requirements for counsel review (TRAK-140).

TRAK-25 defines nine acceptance checks; check 9 was added on 8 October:

1. Create eight weeks of weekly training in under two minutes.
2. Every affected player and parent sees changes/cancellations on next app
   load; the parent's bell includes them. Same-day changes/cancellations also
   reach guardians by email within 60 seconds.
3. Apple, Google and Outlook subscriptions show new, changed and cancelled
   events after refresh without duplicates, tested on real phones.
4. Withdrawal removes the child's events from the app and calendar link on
   the next request.
5. Real-role SQL tests prove there is no cross-academy event visibility.
6. No calendar entry contains a child's name.
7. A past event becomes a completed session with attendance in one step, and
   existing J4/J5 tests stay green.
8. A two-phone production rehearsal with synthetic families and all three
   calendar apps passes before real families receive events.
9. A parent or player subscribes from signup in one tap on iPhone, Android
   with Google Calendar, and Outlook.

Implementation, provider choices and executable operating procedures are not
yet complete. These are required outcomes, not claims that the feature is
available. Follow the open decisions in the 18 issues; do not invent controls,
import parsing or provider behavior. Direct Google/Microsoft calendar account
connections, the academy view, lineups and automatic WhatsApp posting remain out.

## Guarantees

The backend must enforce these. Each requires negative tests under actual
authenticated roles, with controls proving the protected data exists.

- **G1:** no development-record write for an under-18 without active consent,
  across assessments, awards, matches, training and shared feedback. Parked
  features' writes remain closed regardless of consent.
- **G2:** children cannot approve themselves, alter their age or choose their
  guardian's address.
- **G3:** no cross-child or cross-academy access, including the new event paths.
- **G4:** children receive only coach-authored, published content; private notes
  remain private.
- **G5:** no message about a child reaches the wrong adult.
- **G6:** withdrawal stops processing and family access; event/calendar access
  must also satisfy J8's next-request check.
- **G7:** AI processing, player passport/export/sharing, child photos and avatars
  remain disabled in both UI and backend. Account deletion and access to account
  data are separate rights. `export_my_account()` exists; a self-service export
  screen is not established by this audit (TRAK-81).

Tests must include uninvited signup, age changes, name/code linking, writes for
waiting children, private-note reads, wrong-adult delivery and cross-child reads.

## Outside the pilot

Parked screens may retain their design with a Coming soon label, but their
actions send nothing and backend writes stay closed (TRAK-85). The route map
lives in `src/App.tsx`; a rendered screen is not proof its feature is admitted.

The TRAK-43 route decisions remain part of the contract:

- Required paths include admission/authentication, parent consent/profile and
  selected-child history, coach squad/player/assessment/session/profile/manual,
  player home/feedback/history/profile, shared profile/settings and dashboard.
  The new `/auth/code` flow supplements the separate signup confirmation path.
- `/coach/sessions/quick` remains the J4 Match entry and opens the same
  `CoachAddSession` screen as `/coach/sessions/add` in match mode.
- `/player/passport`, `/player/evolution`, `/coach/feedback/:assessmentId`
  (AI draft review), `/coach/assistant`, `/coach/recognition`, `/coach/award`,
  `/parent/alerts` and the `/club/*` console remain Coming soon.
  `/club/home` must render without errors when the academy director signs in.
- `/coach/schedule` remains parked at the audited baseline. TRAK-127 requires
  reusing `CoachSchedule` and enabling that route behind TRAK-124's academy
  switch; this is planned work, not a current deployment claim.
- `/coach/squad/add` and avatar upload stay removed. `/coach/quick-assess`
  stays hidden; its former home/post-match entry points use `/coach/assess`.

AI feedback/assistant/schedule parsing, player passport and sharing, child
photos, recognition/awards, parent goals, player-entered match logging, guardian
invitation of other adults, a household entity, per-child training notes and
academy transfers remain outside the pilot. The academy supplies every guardian;
founders handle exceptional academy changes. Manual retry is sufficient when
the failure is clearly shown.

TRAK-81 records a self-service account-data download as the first feature after
the pilot starts. It remains Backlog; its delivery must also follow the pilot
change policy once agreed. Until it ships, account-data requests use the
authorized support/operator procedure, with scope confirmed by counsel.

Other agreed later scope, without a committed release date or ordering:

- **Matchday (TRAK-123, parked):** yes/no/maybe availability and a lineup from
  available players which players can see. Response-age rules, parent lineup
  visibility and reply deadlines remain open. Kit moved into J8; matchday
  reuses it. Automated WhatsApp reminders are only a later possibility;
  manual event sharing is already planned in J8.
- A coach-only reminder on a player's birthday, using the roster date of birth.
- Preferred foot and player stats entered by the coach; UAE FA syncing is a
  possible later source. No height or weight.

## Success and launch gate

The quality bar is that no required journey fails during the eight weeks. A
workaround may keep support moving but is not a pass; repair and retest the
journey. Value measures and numeric targets are still to be agreed, using the
J7 measurement limitation honestly.

The existing proposed value measures are the share of published messages shown
to players within seven days, and the share of players receiving a published
message every 14 days. Neither has an agreed numeric target; being shown a
message does not establish that it was read.

Real children join only when every gate passes and a founder majority says go:

1. J1–J8 pass on the deployed build on two real phones with synthetic families
   and approved inboxes. Include siblings, withheld consent and withdrawal.
2. G1–G7 and event-isolation/withdrawal checks pass against the deployed backend.
3. The synthetic 25-player rehearsal passes without unexplained errors.
4. The academy pilot agreement is signed and terms are accepted.
5. Counsel approves data roles, notice, withdrawal/deletion and retention,
   including planned event/calendar processing. Pilot plus 90 days remains a
   proposed retention period, not approved policy.
6. Backup and written recovery procedures exist. The recorded 2 October
   decision accepts daily backups retained for seven days, no PITR and an
   unrehearsed restore. The go decision records that limitation and potential
   loss of writes since the latest backup. Recovery duration is unmeasured.
7. The agreed support path is ready: academy staff and founders in a WhatsApp
   group, without parents or children. The first of Tarek, Imad or Kostas to
   read owns the response and fix. The internal aim is two hours; no response time is promised.
   Share only the child's first name and the problem there. Contact details,
   date of birth and child-data screenshots stay out; use a direct call or
   `support@trakfootball.com` for sensitive details.
8. An operator verifies the actual cohort and agreed dates in `pilot_config`,
   with synthetic counting off. Record the check; do not copy a past configuration
   snapshot as current evidence.
9. J8's nine checks, including one-tap setup, three calendar apps and two-phone rehearsal,
   pass before events reach real families. Adding J8 does not inherit earlier
   J1–J7 rehearsal passes.

The service has no separate staging environment. Synthetic rehearsal and pilot
organizations share the system. The pilot disclosure must explain this and the
recovery limitations. As confirmed on 8 October, the academy has not yet been
briefed; a meeting is planned in the coming weeks. Disclosure is pending and
must be recorded when it happens.

For a wrong recipient, consent bypass, cross-child read, safeguarding concern
or stop/delete request, pause the affected scope and new admissions immediately.
A founder majority decides restart, subject to legal duties.

## Decisions still open

- Final start date, cohort count, squad and named operational contacts.
- Numeric value targets and a better measure of whether a player reads feedback.
- Counsel-approved notice, data roles, retention, transfers and rights handling;
  reconciliation of optional consent wording with actual enforcement.
- J8's remaining choices and verified procedures: email sender/provider/region,
  PDF import method, calendar-feed endpoint, player change emails and reliable
  subscription behavior on the supported phones. Decided link revocation and
  off-switch outcomes still need implementation and rehearsal.
- Changes during the pilot: permitted features/fixes, notice, deploy window,
  emergency review rule and duty rota. The 8 October discussion is a proposal;
  existing review/merge requirements continue until a new rule is agreed and
  implemented.

